Recorded so the decision stays visible rather than being rediscovered.

tls => 1 croaks at construction and cert_path is accepted but read by
nothing, which is the honest state and better than the silent cleartext this
replaced. The consequence stands: a tcp:// daemon can only be reached
unencrypted, so any remote daemon is either on a trusted network, behind an
SSH tunnel, or out of reach for this client.

Wiring it means IO::Socket::SSL in Role::HTTP, client certificate handling
(cert.pem, key.pem, ca.pem - the layout cert_path already points at),
verification defaults, and a decision about whether tls => 1 without
certificates means system CAs or an error.

Not urgent while the unix socket covers local Docker and rootless Podman.
Worth stating: it is the one thing keeping this client from talking to a
remote daemon the way the docker CLI does.


--- DECISION 2026-08-27 (maintainer) ---
IN. TLS gets wired: IO::Socket::SSL in Role::HTTP, client certificates from
cert_path (cert.pem / key.pem / ca.pem), and an explicit answer to what
tls => 1 without certificates means. Not deferred any longer.
